  {"id": "127:1", "fails": ["plain"], "remarks": [
    {"words": "os que andam nos seus caminhos", "problem": "With a plural subject ('os que'), a native ear hears 'seus' first as 'their own ways', the opposite of the Latin's 'his ways' (ejus = the Lord's). You only get the right sense on reflection, which is too late when you are reciting it.", "alternative": "os que andam nos caminhos dele"}
  ]},
  {"id": "127:2", "fails": ["native", "sayable"], "remarks": [
    {"words": "Porque comerás", "problem": "Starting on 'Porque' with nothing before it sounds like a clause that has lost its main verb. Spoken Portuguese uses 'pois' at the head of a sentence and keeps the causal link.", "alternative": "Pois comerás dos trabalhos das tuas mãos:"},
    {"words": "bem-aventurado és, e te irá bem", "problem": "The inverted 'bem-aventurado és' is a Latinate order, and 'és, e' makes a hiatus that trips the voice at the comma. The ordinary order is smoother and puts the stress in the right place before the comma.", "alternative": "és bem-aventurado, e te irá bem."}
  ]},
  {"id": "127:3a", "fails": ["native", "sayable"], "remarks": [
    {"words": "A tua esposa, como videira abundante,", "problem": "Without a verb the line hangs, so the reader has to guess what the tone is doing. This half-verse also opens a new stanza, unlike 3b, which the ear can take as a continuation. A supplied present copula takes nothing from the Latin. Supply it in 3b as well so the two halves stay parallel.", "alternative": "A tua esposa é como videira abundante,"},
    {"words": "nos lados da tua casa", "problem": "'Nos lados' sounds colloquial and spatial ('lá pelos lados de…'), and 'lados da' runs together. The Latin image is the sides or inner walls of the house, and a nobler equivalent that still means 'sides' keeps it without resolving anything.", "alternative": "aos lados da tua casa"}
  ]},
  {"id": "127:3b", "fails": ["native"], "remarks": [
    {"words": "Os teus filhos, como rebentos de oliveira,", "problem": "This has the same verbless hang as 3a. If 3a gets its copula, this line must match it, or the pair stops being parallel.", "alternative": "Os teus filhos são como rebentos de oliveira,"}
  ]},
  {"id": "127:5", "fails": ["plain", "sayable"], "remarks": [
    {"words": "e vejas as coisas boas de Jerusalém", "problem": "'As coisas boas' is flat, and it adds syllables to what is already the longest colon in the psalm. That colon is hard to say in one breath. 'Os bens' is the plainer and nobler faithful word for 'bona', and it saves three syllables.", "alternative": "e vejas os bens de Jerusalém todos os dias da tua vida."}
  ]}
],
"overall": "The psalm is sober and prayable, and it follows the Latin closely without sounding stiff; 127:4 and 127:6 have exactly the simple nobility that is wanted. The weak points are small Latinate habits: the bare 'Porque' at the start of a sentence, the inverted 'bem-aventurado és', verbless comparisons that hang, and one ambiguous 'seus'. Fixing these would make it sound written in Portuguese throughout.",
"worstLine": "127:3a",
"bestLine": "127:6"}
